<p class="gna-lede">Septoplasty is an operation inside the nose that straightens the septum, the wall between your nostrils, so that you can breathe more easily; it normally does not change how your nose looks. Rhinoplasty changes the shape of the outer nose, for appearance, for breathing, or both. When the two are done together it is called septorhinoplasty, and the distinction matters for your recovery and, in India, for insurance.</p>

<h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
<p>If your main problem is a blocked nose caused by a bent septum, septoplasty is usually the relevant operation. If your main concern is the shape of your nose, rhinoplasty is. If your nose is both crooked and blocked, often after an old injury, septorhinoplasty addresses both in one sitting.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Septoplasty is about function: it is done inside the nose and the outward shape normally stays the same.</li>
    <li>Rhinoplasty is about shape, and sometimes function: it reshapes the bone and cartilage of the outer nose.</li>
    <li>Septorhinoplasty combines both and is common for crooked noses and injuries.</li>
    <li>Not every blocked nose needs surgery; allergies, swollen turbinates and overuse of decongestant drops are often treated with medicines first.</li>
    <li>Insurers usually consider documented breathing surgery, but surgery purely to change appearance is generally excluded.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="what-is-septoplasty">What is septoplasty?</h2>
<p>The septum is made of flexible cartilage at the front and thin bone further back, covered on both sides by a moist lining. Very few septums are perfectly straight. A <strong>deviated septum</strong> is one that bends far enough to narrow one or both airways. It can be present from birth, develop as the face grows, or follow an injury, sometimes a childhood fall that nobody remembers.</p>
<p>A significant deviation can cause:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A nose that feels blocked all the time, often worse on one side</li>
  <li>Mouth-breathing, a dry mouth on waking and disturbed sleep</li>
  <li>Snoring, or difficulty tolerating a CPAP mask</li>
  <li>Nosebleeds or crusting on one side</li>
  <li>Recurrent sinus problems, in some people</li>
</ul>
<p>During septoplasty, usually under general anaesthesia, the surgeon works through the nostril. The lining is lifted, the bent cartilage and bone are trimmed, straightened or repositioned, and the lining is laid back down. Soft splints or light packing may be placed for a few days. The operation is often combined with reduction of the turbinates, the ridges on the side walls of the nose that can swell and add to blockage. Most people go home the same day or the next morning, with no external splint and little or no visible bruising.</p>
<p>The nose feels stuffy for one to two weeks while the lining heals, and breathing continues to improve over the following weeks. Good-quality research, including a large randomised trial in the UK, suggests that for adults with a deviated septum and persistent blockage, septoplasty improves symptoms more than nasal sprays alone, although not every patient improves and results vary.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-rhinoplasty">What is rhinoplasty?</h2>
<p>Rhinoplasty reshapes the outer nose by altering its bone and cartilage. It can reduce a hump, refine or support the tip, straighten or narrow the nose, correct asymmetry, change its length, or build up a low bridge using cartilage grafts taken from the septum, ear or rib. It is done either through incisions inside the nostrils (closed rhinoplasty) or with an additional small incision across the skin between the nostrils (open rhinoplasty), which gives the surgeon a direct view of the framework.</p>
<p>Rhinoplasty is not only cosmetic. <strong>Functional rhinoplasty</strong> corrects problems of the outer nose that block breathing, such as a narrow or collapsing nasal valve, the narrowest part of the airway just inside the nostrils. If the sides of your nose suck inwards when you breathe in, straightening the septum alone may not help, and valve support may be needed.</p>
<p>Recovery is longer than after septoplasty: an external splint stays on for about a week, bruising around the eyes usually fades over two to three weeks, and swelling settles gradually over many months, with the final tip shape taking up to a year or more. Our <a href="/blogs/rhinoplasty-recovery-timeline-swelling-guide/">rhinoplasty recovery timeline</a> goes through this week by week.</p>

<h2 id="septorhinoplasty">What is septorhinoplasty?</h2>
<p>Septorhinoplasty is a combined operation that straightens the septum and reshapes the outer nose. It is often the right approach when:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The nose is visibly crooked, because a deviated septum frequently pulls the outer nose to one side</li>
  <li>There has been an injury that changed both the shape of the nose and your breathing</li>
  <li>You are troubled by both breathing and appearance and would prefer one anaesthetic and one recovery</li>
  <li>Septal cartilage is needed as graft material to support or rebuild the nose</li>
</ul>
<p>If your nose was recently broken, timing changes the options; see <a href="/blogs/broken-nose-when-does-it-need-surgery/">when a broken nose needs surgery</a>.</p>

<h2 id="comparison">Septoplasty vs rhinoplasty at a glance</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th></th><th>Septoplasty</th><th>Rhinoplasty</th><th>Septorhinoplasty</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Main purpose</td><td>Breathing</td><td>Shape, sometimes breathing</td><td>Both</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Changes outward appearance?</td><td>Not normally</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Incisions</td><td>Inside the nostril</td><td>Inside the nostrils, with or without a small cut between them</td><td>As for rhinoplasty</td></tr>
    <tr><td>External splint</td><td>Usually none</td><td>About one week</td><td>About one week</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Visible bruising</td><td>Little or none</td><td>Common, around the eyes for 2–3 weeks</td><td>Common</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Typical time off work</td><td>About one week</td><td>One to two weeks</td><td>One to two weeks</td></tr>
    <tr><td>When the result is clear</td><td>Breathing improves over weeks to a few months</td><td>Around a year, longer for the tip</td><td>Around a year</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Insurance (India)</td><td>Often covered when medically necessary</td><td>Cosmetic surgery generally excluded</td><td>Functional or post-accident part may be considered</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>These are typical figures; your own recovery depends on what is done, your skin and healing, and whether other procedures are combined.</p>

