<h2 id="which-do-you-need">Which operation do you need?</h2>
<p>The starting point is a simple question: what bothers you most, your breathing, the way your nose looks, or both? The answer, together with an examination, decides the plan.</p>
<p>At a consultation we ask about your symptoms, allergies, any nasal sprays you use and previous injuries. We examine the outer nose and the inside of the nose, check whether the nasal valves collapse when you breathe in, and may arrange a nasal endoscopy (a thin camera examination) or a CT scan if sinus disease is suspected. For changes in shape, standardised photographs help us plan and discuss what is realistic.</p>
<h3>When surgery is not the first step</h3>
<p>A blocked nose has many causes besides a bent septum. Allergic rhinitis is common in Hyderabad, where dust and pollen are year-round triggers and symptoms often flare in the monsoon. Swollen turbinates, nasal polyps and sinus infections all cause blockage. So does overuse of decongestant nasal drops, which are widely available over the counter: used for more than a few days, they can cause rebound congestion. These problems are usually treated first with steroid sprays, saline rinses and allergy management, and surgery is considered only if symptoms persist.</p>
<p>Nasal blockage can also worsen snoring, but the throat is usually the main source. If snoring is your main concern, read about <a href="/blogs/snoring-vs-sleep-apnea-difference/">the difference between snoring and sleep apnoea</a> before planning nasal surgery.</p>
<h3>Realistic expectations for shape</h3>
<p>Rhinoplasty can refine a nose and bring it into better balance with the rest of the face, but it works with your existing skin, bone and cartilage. Thicker nasal skin, which many Indian patients have, limits how sharply defined a tip can become. A good plan aims for a nose that looks natural on your face, not a copy of someone else's.</p>

<h2 id="insurance">Insurance: why the difference matters</h2>
<p>In India, most health insurance policies exclude cosmetic or plastic surgery done to change appearance, unless it is reconstruction after an accident, burns or cancer, or is needed to treat a direct health risk. In practice this means:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Septoplasty</strong> for a documented breathing problem is generally regarded as medically necessary. Many policies cover it, but deviated nasal septum is often listed with a specific waiting period from the start of the policy, so check your policy wording.</li>
  <li><strong>Rhinoplasty purely for appearance</strong> is usually self-funded.</li>
  <li><strong>Septorhinoplasty</strong> may be approved for its functional part, or in full if it reconstructs a nose damaged in an accident; the cosmetic element is usually not covered.</li>
</ul>
<p>Decisions are made by the insurer or its third-party administrator through pre-authorisation, not by the hospital. Insurers typically ask for a record of your symptoms and the medicines you have tried, examination and endoscopy findings, any CT scan, and, after an accident, the records from the time of injury. Cashless treatment may be available at our NABH-accredited partner hospitals, subject to your policy terms and pre-authorisation.</p>

<h2 id="risks">Risks to know about</h2>
<p>Both operations are commonly performed and usually go smoothly, but no surgery is free of risk.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Septoplasty:</strong> bleeding, infection, a blood collection on the septum, a small hole in the septum (perforation), blockage that persists or returns, temporary numbness of the upper front teeth and, rarely, a change in smell or in the support of the nose.</li>
  <li><strong>Rhinoplasty and septorhinoplasty:</strong> the risks above, plus swelling that lasts many months, asymmetry, breathing difficulty, visible irregularities, a scar between the nostrils after open surgery that usually fades well, and dissatisfaction with the result. A proportion of patients choose a revision operation.</li>
</ul>
<p>Results and recovery vary from person to person. For a wider perspective on planning nasal surgery, see <a href="/blogs/rhinoplasty-balancing-aesthetics-function/">balancing aesthetics and function in rhinoplasty</a>.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>It is worth seeing a specialist if your nose has felt blocked for more than three months despite sprays, if one side is persistently blocked with bleeding or foul-smelling discharge (which always needs examination), if your nose became crooked after an injury, or if you are considering a change in shape and want honest advice about what is achievable.</p>
